## Bug report

**Required Info:**

- Operating System:
  - Ubuntu 24.04
- Installation type:
  - binaries
- Version or commit hash:
  - Jazzy: rclcpp 28.1.23, rcl 9.2.11
- DDS implementation:
  - rmw_cyclonedds_cpp
- Client library (if applicable):
  - rclcpp

#### Steps to reproduce issue

A single user thread creates and destroys clients on a node that a `MultiThreadedExecutor` is spinning. The user code never touches the node from more than one thread.

```cpp
#include <chrono>
#include <cstdio>
#include <memory>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>

#include <rclcpp/rclcpp.hpp>
#include <std_srvs/srv/empty.hpp>
#include <std_srvs/srv/set_bool.hpp>
#include <std_srvs/srv/trigger.hpp>

int main(int argc, char** argv) {
  rclcpp::init(argc, argv);
  auto node = std::make_shared<rclcpp::Node>("type_cache_repro");

  rclcpp::executors::MultiThreadedExecutor executor(rclcpp::ExecutorOptions(), 4);
  executor.add_node(node);
  std::thread spin_thread([&executor]() { executor.spin(); });

  const auto deadline = std::chrono::steady_clock::now() + std::chrono::seconds(60);
  long iterations = 0;
  while (std::chrono::steady_clock::now() < deadline) {
    std::vector<rclcpp::ClientBase::SharedPtr> clients;
    clients.push_back(node->create_client<std_srvs::srv::SetBool>("set_bool"));
    clients.push_back(node->create_client<std_srvs::srv::Trigger>("trigger"));
    clients.push_back(node->create_client<std_srvs::srv::Empty>("empty"));
    clients.clear();
    ++iterations;
  }
  std::printf("no crash after %ld iterations\n", iterations);

  executor.cancel();
  spin_thread.join();
  rclcpp::shutdown();
  return 0;
}
```

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.16)
project(type_cache_repro CXX)
set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 17)
find_package(rclcpp REQUIRED)
find_package(std_srvs REQUIRED)
add_executable(repro repro.cpp)
target_link_libraries(repro rclcpp::rclcpp ${std_srvs_TARGETS})
```

#### Expected behavior

Runs for 60 s and prints `no crash after N iterations`.

#### Actual behavior

It crashes before the deadline. Backtrace (via `libbackward.so`):

```
#11 repro.cpp:31 in clear
#10 shared_ptr_base.h:199 in _M_release_last_use
#9  destroy<rclcpp::Client<std_srvs::srv::Empty> >
#8  librclcpp.so
#7  librclcpp.so
#6  librcl.so in rcl_client_fini
#5  librcl.so in rcl_node_type_cache_unregister_type
#4  type_description_interfaces__msg__TypeDescription__destroy
#3  type_description_interfaces__msg__TypeDescription__fini
#2  type_description_interfaces__msg__Field__Sequence__fini
#1  type_description_interfaces__msg__Field__fini
#0  rosidl_runtime_c__String__fini
Segmentation fault (Address not mapped to object [0x31])
```

In a larger application, the same race shows up as glibc's `double free or corruption (!prev)`. There, the frames have the executor thread finalizing a client inside `Executor::wait_for_work`, while another thread runs `create_client` on the same node:

```
rclcpp::executors::MultiThreadedExecutor::run
  rclcpp::Executor::get_next_executable
    rclcpp::Executor::wait_for_work
      ~Client<std_srvs::srv::SetBool>
        rcl_client_fini
          rcl_node_type_cache_unregister_type
            rcutils_hash_map_set
              free -> abort
```

#### Additional information

- `WaitSetTemplate::wait()` takes shared ownership of every entity for the duration of the wait (`storage_acquire_ownerships()` / `RCPPUTILS_SCOPE_EXIT(storage_release_ownerships())`). So when the user drops the last reference to a client while an executor thread is waiting, the executor thread runs `rcl_client_fini` when its wait returns. Otherwise the user's thread does. The user has no control over which thread finalizes the entity.
- `rcl_client_init`, `rcl_client_fini` (and the matching service, publisher and subscription calls) register and unregister the type in the node's type cache (`rcl_node_type_cache_register_type` / `_unregister_type`). Both write the node's `rcutils_hash_map` without synchronization. rcl documents these functions as `Thread-Safe: No`.
- So rclcpp calls the init and fini functions concurrently on the same node, although rcl says they are not thread-safe, and the application cannot prevent it by serializing its own calls.
- Looking at the source, the rcl type cache code and the wait set ownership handling are unchanged on `rolling`, so this probably also affects Kilted and Rolling. I have only run the reproducer on Jazzy.
- Possible fixes: a per-node mutex in rclcpp held around every `rcl_*_init` / `rcl_*_fini` that touches the type cache, or synchronization inside `rcl_node_type_cache_*`.
